Q1 – Lists and Slicing: Daily Rainfall Records (6 Marks)
1. Program: weekly totals, extremes, moving averages and dry days
def rainfall_report(readings):
    weekly_totals = [sum(readings[i:i+7]) for i in range(0, len(readings), 7)]
    if readings:
        wettest_day = (max(readings), readings.index(max(readings)))
        driest_day = (min(readings), readings.index(min(readings)))
    else:
        wettest_day = driest_day = None
    moving = [sum(readings[i:i+7])/7
              for i in range(max(0, len(readings)-6))]
    dry_days = [i+1 for i, value in enumerate(readings) if value == 0]
    return weekly_totals, wettest_day, driest_day, moving, dry_days
# Example (365 values)
rain = [float((i * 7) % 19) for i in range(365)]
weekly, wettest, driest, moving, dry = rainfall_report(rain)
print("weeks:", len(weekly), "last week days:", len(rain) % 7 or 7)
print("wettest reading/index:", wettest)
print("driest reading/index:", driest)
print("moving averages:", len(moving))
print("dry days:", dry[:10])
Weekly totals use slices of seven days; the final slice may contain fewer than seven readings if the list length is not a multiple of
seven. A 365-day year therefore has 52 complete weeks and one final day.
2. Pseudocode and slicing
Moving-average pseudocode: if fewer than 7 readings, return an empty result; otherwise, for each start position from 0 to n-7, add
the seven readings and divide by 7.
readings[7:14] returns items at indexes 7 through 13 (the second block of seven). readings[-7:] returns the final seven items.
readings[::7] returns every seventh item, starting at index 0; it does not calculate weekly totals.
3. List operations and complexity
A list is suitable because readings are ordered by day, supports indexing and slicing, and grows dynamically. append is O(1)
amortised; insert at an arbitrary position is O(n) because later elements shift; pop() at the end is O(1), while pop(i) is O(n); sort is O(n
log n).
Naive moving average performs 7 additions for each of n-6 windows: 7(n-6), which is O(7n)=O(n). A running-sum method uses 7
initial additions and two arithmetic updates per next window (subtract the leaving value and add the entering value), giving O(n) with
fewer operations.
