5. Late reading and alternatives
Inserting a late reading at its date in a Python list costs O(n) due to shifting. A list remains suitable for a small or moderate yearly
dataset and simple sequential reports. For frequent insertions at both ends, deque is useful; for date-keyed random access or
missing dates, a dictionary may be appropriate.
A deque(maxlen=7) can maintain a rolling seven-day window; update a running sum as old readings leave and new ones enter. This
is O(n) total time and O(7)=O(1) window space. A full date dictionary provides average O(1) lookup by date but needs O(n) storage
and extra date-key management.
