2. Tuple keys, copying and mutability
A tuple containing only hashable immutable values can be a dictionary key because its hash remains stable. A list is mutable and
unhashable, so using [13.0418, 80.2341] as a key raises TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'.
Memory concept: bad = [[0]*3]*3 creates one inner list referenced by all three outer positions. The corrected comprehension creates
three distinct inner lists. In a shallow copy, the outer container is new but nested objects are shared. A deep copy recursively copies
nested objects.
original = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]
alias = original
shallow1 = copy.copy(original)
shallow2 = original[:]
deep = copy.deepcopy(original)
print(alias is original)             # True
print(shallow1 is original)          # False
print(shallow1[0] is original[0])    # True
print(deep[0] is original[0])        # False
def add_value(values):
    values.append(99)
data = [1, 2]
add_value(data)
print(data)                          # [1, 2, 99]
mixed = ([1, 2], "bus")
mixed[0].append(3)                   # allowed: inner list is mutable
print(mixed)                         # ([1, 2, 3], 'bus')
A shallow copy is enough when the contents are immutable or when sharing nested objects is intended. Deep copy is needed when
independent nested mutable objects are required. A function receives a reference to the same list object, so mutating it changes
what the caller sees.
