2. Comprehension concepts
General form: [expression for item in iterable if condition]. A comprehension becomes harder to read when it contains multiple nested
loops, several conditions or complex transformations; a normal loop is clearer then.
# Dictionary comprehension: minute -> valid Celsius reading
valid_by_minute = {i: c for i, c in enumerate(readings, 1)
                   if -50 <= c <= 50}
# Set comprehension: distinct valid Celsius readings
valid_unique = {c for c in readings if -50 <= c <= 50}
# Generator expression: values are produced lazily
valid_generator = (c for c in readings if -50 <= c <= 50)
A list comprehension immediately builds and stores a list (O(n) memory). A generator expression yields values on demand and
usually uses O(1) extra iteration memory, though it can only be consumed once.
